<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Divine Comedy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1308–1321</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Commedia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Divine Comedy is a long narrative poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a>, written in the Florentine vernacular from about 1308 until his death in 1321. In three parts, Inferno, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purgatorio">Purgatorio</a> and Paradiso, it describes the poet's journey through Hell, Purgatory and Heaven, guided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> and Beatrice, and is filled with figures from Florentine and Italian politics. It is regarded as the greatest work of Italian literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/divine-comedy.jpg" alt="Divine Comedy" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Domenico di Michelino / After Alesso Baldovinetti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dante_Domenico_di_Michelino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
